<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="weaving-model" title="The weaving model">

    <show-structure for="chapter" depth="2"/>

    <p>
        A weave is a declaration, and the engine treats it as one. It reads your annotations once
        and folds every weave of the run into a single plan. Every class a driver offers is then
        answered from that plan.
    </p>

    <code-block lang="mermaid">
flowchart TB
    A["Parser reads your @Weave class"] --> C["Declarations: targets, handlers, members"]
    C --> D["Planner orders and compares"]
    D --> E["One plan, keyed by internal name"]
    F["A class offered by a driver"] --> G{"Named in the plan?"}
    E --> G
    G -->|no| H["Left unwoven"]
    G -->|yes| I["Injections, then merging"]
    I --> J["Rewritten bytes"]
    </code-block>

    <chapter title="What a weave hands over" id="declares">
        <p>
            The parse builds an eleven-part <code>WeaveClass</code>. This page follows three of
            them: targets, handlers and members.
        </p>

        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="Targets">
                <p>
                    The classes the weave claims, each recorded with the spelling you used. A
                    weave needs at least one; <code>require()</code> only decides whether a
                    missing one fails the build, and nothing past the parse reads it. Any error
                    during the parse throws the whole weave away, never half of it.
                    <a href="annotations.topic">The annotation reference</a> has every element of
                    <code>@Weave</code>.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Handlers">
                <p>
                    Your methods carrying <code>@Inject</code>, <code>@Redirect</code> or
                    <code>@Wrap</code>, each paired with the site it claims. A handler is never
                    also a member. <a href="handlers-and-callbacks.topic">Handlers and callbacks</a> covers what one
                    may take and return.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Members">
                <p>
                    Fields and methods in one of four shapes: merged, shadowed, accessor, invoker.
                    The shape decides what the merge stage does with the name.
                    <a href="weave-members.topic">Weave members</a> has the four.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Three moments, three views" id="moments">
        <p>
            Each moment sees more than the one before it. That decides where a rule can be
            enforced at all.
        </p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr>
                <td>Moment</td>
                <td>What it reads</td>
                <td>What it settles</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>The parse</td>
                <td>one weave's class file</td>
                <td>its targets, handlers and members</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Planning</td>
                <td>every weave of the run</td>
                <td>order, and conflicting declarations</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Weaving</td>
                <td>the target, and again for merging</td>
                <td>whether every name resolves</td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <p>
            No target is read until the third. So planning compares the text you wrote. Two
            weaves that name one call site in different words never look like one claim.
            <a href="ordering.topic">Ordering</a> covers priority and the tie-breakers behind it.
        </p>

        <note>
            <p>
                A contradiction found during planning does not stop the plan. Every pass runs and
                reports, so one build shows all of them instead of one per rebuild. Two weaves
                merging one member into one class is <code>AW1080</code>. Rename all but one.
                Marking them <code>@Unique</code> silences the code without separating the
                names: a member is <a href="weave-members.topic">mangled</a> only where the
                target declares that name itself.
            </p>
        </note>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Dissolved, or left standing" id="dissolve">
        <p>
            The plan asks one question of each weave. Both halves of it count.
        </p>

        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="An instance weave with a member or an injector">
                <p>
                    It dissolves into every target it names. A target is rebuilt only where the
                    weave has something to contribute; a plain <code>@Shadow</code> alone changes
                    nothing.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="An instance weave with neither">
                <p>
                    It reaches none of its targets. Declaring a target and nothing else is valid
                    — and does nothing.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="A static weave">
                <p>
                    Never dissolves, whatever it declares. Members it writes are modelled and
                    never moved. Only its injections change a target.
                    <a href="weave-members.topic">Weave members</a> says which shapes are worth declaring
                    there.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>

        <p>
            The plan indexes dissolving weaves separately from injections, both keyed by
            internal name — the slash-separated form the class file itself uses. That second
            index is why a weave which only merges still reaches its targets.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Declarations, and no bodies" id="bodies">
        <p>
            A member records a name, a type and its flags. It never records code. Nothing in the
            model holds a parsed class.
        </p>

        <p>
            A stage that has to move a body therefore reads the weave's class file a second time.
            Where nothing supplies it, merging refuses with <code>AW1096</code>. Give the weaver a
            source for the weave's own bytes; <a href="choose-a-driver.topic">each driver wires
            that up its own way</a>.
            <a href="diagnostics.topic">Diagnostics</a> explains the ranges.
        </p>

        <tip>
            <p>
                An accessor and an invoker are built onto the target from the declaration alone.
                A weave of nothing but those needs no class file at all.
            </p>
        </tip>
    </chapter>

